AB  - The rapid adoption of large language models (LLMs) in real-world applications has been driven by two dominant tuning paradigms: Instruction tuning and Chat tuning. Instruction-tuned models, such as FLAN-T5 and LLaMA2-Instruct, are designed to follow specific task instructions, while chat-tuned models like ChatGPT and LLaMA2-Chat are optimized for multi-turn conversation. This paper presents a systematic comparative study of these two paradigms across a diverse set of NLP tasks, including question answering, summarization, code generation, safety, and dialogue coherence. We benchmark the performance using both automatic evaluation metrics and human preferences, using datasets like MT-Bench, TruthfulQA, AlpacaEval, and SafeBench. Our results reveal that instruction-tuned models perform better on single-turn tasks with clearer prompts, while chat-tuned models exhibit superior adaptability and engagement in dialogue settings. We also identify trade-offs in terms of hallucination, verbosity, and ethical alignment. This study offers practical guidelines for researchers and developers to select appropriate tuning strategies for their use cases and proposes a hybrid training pipeline for future models.
ER  - 

