AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently demonstrated strong capabilities in code generation and problem solving, which opens new opportunities for intelligent decisionmaking. In this paper, we propose MuEvoLLM, an algorithm integrating LLMs with evolutionary strategies to automatically generate and evolve interpretable control policies for MuJoCo benchmarks. The framework applies semantic mutation operators guided by LLM prompts, evaluates candidate policies in simulation, and selects the best-performing strategies in an elitist manner. We validate our approach on the Walker2d-v4 environment and compare against two strong baselines, Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) and Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO). Experimental results show that MuEvoLLM achieves faster convergence, more stable improvements, and higher final rewards, highlighting the potential of LLM-driven algorithm design in reinforcement learning.
