AB  - Large language model (LLM) hallucinations in structured output generation-code, schemas, and query languages-are qualitatively distinct from natural language hallucinations: they are mechanistically predictable, formally verifiable, and classifiable against the constraint space of the target domain. Existing frameworks measure hallucination as a scalar rate, without distinguishing failure mechanisms or informing repair. We present ConstraintKG, a knowledge graph (KG) framework that encodes domain constraint spaces as typed directed graphs and automatically classifies LLM outputs into three failure families via KG traversal: Type 1 (Schema Violations, $\mathbf{T}_{1}$), Type 2 (Semantic Violations, $\mathbf{T}_{2}$), and Type 3 (Cross-element Constraint Violations, $\mathbf{T}_{3}$). We introduce a formal detection operator $\delta_{\mathcal{G}}$ and a type-conditioned repair operator $\hat{\rho}$ grounded in KG edge semantics. Evaluation across 420 GPT-4omini outputs (360 standard, 60 adversarial $\mathbf{T}_{2}$) spanning three structured domains (JSON Schema, SQL, Code Generation) yields an overall hallucination rate of 10.6% on the standard corpus, with Code reaching 30.0%. Type-conditioned repair achieves 66.7% overall Repair Success Rate (RSR) versus 35.6% for generic re-prompting, with $\mathbf{T}_{1}$ at $68.6\%[n=35]$ and $\mathbf{T}_{2}$ at $60 \%[n=5]$ - showing that failure type informs repair strategy on mid-capability models. On a stronger model (GPT-4o) the typed/generic gap closes (68.9% vs. 68.9%), indicating the benefit is capability-dependent rather than universal. The framework formally grounds the three-type taxonomy within Orthogonal Defect Classification [12].
