AB  - Sixteen postgraduate researchers submitted an identical prompt to Gemini 3.1 during a hands-on web development workshop and received outputs that differed across five dimensions: how the model opened its response, which packages it chose, how many domain tables it generated (range: 4 to 18), whether the application ran on the first attempt (4 of 16 sessions), and how it resolved a role-count contradiction embedded in the prompt. No session implemented exactly the three roles specified; every session silently implemented four or five instead. We document this variation with a five-category coding scheme grounded in submitted artifacts and post-workshop survey responses. The central finding is that identical prompts produce unequal and invisible starting positions for non-technical users, who lack the background to detect or compensate for the differences they receive. We argue that tools for non-technical LLM users need built-in structural output comparison to make this variation visible.
ER  - 

