AB  - LLMs (Large language models) are becoming increasingly popular in engineering design tasks due to their excellent semantic understanding and code generation capabilities. However, there is a lack of effective interaction specifications for human-LLM collaboration in engineering design, leading to an overabundance of ineffective and aimless human-LLM dialogues, as well as underperformance of LLMs in certain complex engineering design tasks. To address these limitations, this paper presents a human-LLM interaction framework by standardizing prompt and incorporating human knowledge into LLMs. The prompt paradigm consists of three parts: referenceable examples, expected tasks, and output requirements. An error correction mechanism is suggested to rectify errors based on human knowledge. A case study on CAD and CAE is provided to illustrate the implementation of the human-LLM interaction framework in engineering design. Experimental results show that the proposed framework can significantly assist in solving engineering design tasks.
