AB  - Large language models (LLMs) show considerable potential for controlling electromagnetic (EM) simulation software through scripting interfaces such as the visual basic for applications (VBAs) application programming interface (API). However, direct code generation is vulnerable to hallucination, whereby the model produces syntactically plausible but incorrect or nonexecutable commands because of insufficient grounding in software-specific interfaces and operation requirements. To mitigate this issue, this article proposes a function tool-calling framework in which frequently used modelling and simulation operations are encapsulated as predefined functions with structured schemas and verified local VBA implementations. The LLM is therefore responsible for selecting the intended function and generating all required arguments rather than producing arbitrary VBA code. The developed tool library supports geometric modelling, parameter adjustment, unit-cell simulation configuration, solver execution, and simulation data extraction, enabling reliable parameterized simulation and structured data acquisition through natural-language interaction. Furthermore, a lightweight hierarchical multiagent strategy with a mode-switching mechanism is introduced for complex tasks. It separates common-mode and array-mode function sets and automatically augments array-related prompts, thereby reducing interference from irrelevant candidate tools. The proposed framework is evaluated using ten models from OpenAI, Qwen, and Anthropic over 720 common-mode and 390 array-mode test cases, where a result is considered correct only when both the intended function and all required arguments are correctly returned. In common mode, the Claude models achieve accuracies of 98.89%–99.58%, the OpenAI models achieve 97.36%–99.03%, and the Qwen models achieve 94.31%–96.39%. In array mode, most models maintain accuracies above 95%, while the distractor-tool experiment demonstrates that enlarging the candidate tool set can cause strongly model-dependent degradation. A direct zero-shot VBA comparison further shows that unconstrained prompting produces VBA-like outputs for most requests but yields task-level accuracies of only 17.2%, 2.7%, and 11.8% for representative OpenAI, Qwen, and Claude models, respectively. Practical polarization rotation surface (PRS) and 2-bit RIS cases are used to validate unit-cell modelling, parameter adjustment, simulation execution, data extraction, and array construction. To further demonstrate simulation-data-acquisition capability, limited-iteration parameter-search demonstrations are conducted by constraining the LLM to the predefined function tools. Within a 100-iteration search budget, the PRS case obtains a best contiguous bandwidth of 0.855 GHz above the $-1.0$ dB threshold, corresponding to 28.5% of the 6–9 GHz sweep range, while the 2-bit RIS case evaluates four diode-state phase responses at 7 GHz and obtains a best maximum phase error of 39.95° among the tested parameter sets. The number of API requests, token usage, and representative response time are also reported for the simulation-data-acquisition workflow to characterize its implementation overhead. These results demonstrate that the proposed framework substantially improves the reliability of LLM-assisted EM modelling and provides a practical tool-constrained interface for acquiring full-wave simulation data for subsequent data-driven processing.
ER  - 

