AB  - The ever-growing scale and intricacy of cloud-based artificial intelligence platforms have made guaranteeing service dependability through swift fault identification and flexible restoration an increasingly pressing concern. Although contemporary techniques combine Large Language Models (LLMs) to capture semantic meaning alongside Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) to refine decision policies, most implementations depend on cascaded, weakly-integrated structures that fail to fully exploit the creative generation and deductive strengths inherent to LLMs. This work introduces PASE (Planning-Aware Semantic self-healing engine), an innovative autonomous repair framework that fundamentally reinterprets recovery as a neuro-symbolic program synthesis challenge. Within PASE, an LLM functions as the central Plan Synthesis Engine, constructing structured remediation schemes drawn from a collection of fundamental operations. Meanwhile, a Neural-Symbolic World Model assesses plan viability through predictive simulation, complemented by a Meta-Prompt Optimizer that leverages DRL training to acquire the ability to produce superior prompts directing the LLM’s reasoning workflow. This cohesive "reason-plan-verify-adapt" cycle facilitates the generation of dynamic, situationally-appropriate recovery strategies that transcend conventional action libraries. Evaluations conducted using authentic cloud fault injection data reveal that PASE achieves substantial performance gains compared to leading existing techniques, decreasing mean system restoration duration by greater than 40% while enhancing fault identification precision for previously unencountered failure scenarios. Our methodology propels forward autonomous system administration by synthesizing LLM-driven inference, simulation-based validation, and meta-learned steering into a unified architecture.
