AB  - System software is highly complex and requires significant manual development effort. With the wide application of large language models (LLMs) in software engineering, LLMs are increasingly used to automatically generate system software at the function level. However, due to their limited accuracy, the generated functions often contain errors, which prevent them from being compiled and executed for regression testing. As a result, developers must manually debug these functions, which is time-consuming and heavily dependent on expertise. In this paper, we first emphasize the emerging need for assessing function correctness without relying on regression tests in the context of LLM-generated system software. Next, we propose a preliminary data-driven approach, TeleSys, which generates a substantial amount of function variants (covering correct and erroneous functions) to fine-tune LLMs for correctness assessment. Preliminary results show that an LLM (QWen2.5-Coder-1.5B) fine-tuned with TeleSys-generated data outperforms two prompt-based baselines (ICE-Score and SWE-Judge, combined with QWen2.5-Coder-32B and GPT-4o-mini), demonstrating the effectiveness of our data-driven approach in improving the accuracy of correctness assessment for LLM-generated system software. Finally, we propose two future directions to enable more effective deployment of LLMs for system software correctness assessment in practical scenarios.
