AB  - Due to the extremely strict requirements for safety and compliance, the automatic generation of codes for aviation safety-critical software still faces challenges. Although large language models (LLMs) demonstrate amazing progress in code generation, the codes they produce often fail to meet essential safety criteria. Such codes may lack traceability to requirements, compliance with filed regulations, and alignment with established engineering practices—each of these being critical in safety-critical domains. This paper introduces KNSE-Code, a knowledge-guided framework designed to integrate formally verified engineering knowledge directly into the code generation process. By dynamically injecting structured rules from a domain-specific knowledge graph into the prompts, the framework ensures that safety standards are inherently respected during code generation, rather than through post-hoc inspection. Three software requirements from a safety-critical aircraft system, which span the domains of mode transition, control logic, and data processing, were selected for the validation of the KNSE model. comparing it against manual coding and direct LLM generation. The results show that KNSE-Code matches 100% functional complete, reduces safety violations by up to 85% compared to direct LLM generation, and completely avoids high-severity violations. The generated code is fully traceable and annotated, supporting auditability and long-term maintainability. This approach highlights the value of integrating domain knowledge with LLMs to build trustworthy, compliant, and maintainable software for safety-critical domains.
