AB  - Finite State Machines (FSMs), typically implemented in Verilog, are fundamental to the control logic of Systems-on-Chip (SoCs). With recent advances in large language models (LLMs) for code generation, LLMs have been increasingly explored for Verilog code generation. However, LLM-generated Verilog code frequently contains security vulnerabilities, which is particularly concerning for security-critical FSMs designs. To address this issue, we propose SecFSM, a method that leverages a security-oriented knowledge graph to guide LLMs in generating more secure Verilog code. Specifically, we first construct a FSM Security Knowledge Graph (FSKG), which is a FSM-oriented, multi-layer knowledge schema that organizes security knowledge across three levels, i.e., FSM vulnerability, vulnerability semantics, and mitigation and secure design patterns. Given user inputs consisting of natural-language requirements, we then construct a structured FSM graph with explicit security semantics. On this model, we design a two-stage vulnerability analysis algorithm: (i) structured FSM graph analysis to identify structured and potential vulnerabilities, and (ii) knowledge-guided vulnerability confirmation through semantic reasoning over FSKG. The resulting structured vulnerability knowledge, explicitly aligned with FSKG, is finally incorporated into an FSM-structure-aware, three-stage security prompting template to guide security-constrained Verilog FSM generation. To evaluate SecFSM, we build a dataset of 52 test cases collected from multiple sources. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SecFSM consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines (RAG and base LLM models) across all evaluators. Specifically, SecFSM achieves a pass rate of 48/52 with DeepSeek-R1, 46/52 with Claude 3.5, and 39/52 with GPT-4o across 52 test cases. In stark contrast, the RAG and base LLM baselines only pass up to 19/52 and 19/52 cases, respectively, demonstrating SecFSM's superior capability in robust security enforcement.
