AB  - Traditional train trajectory evaluation methods suffer from subjectivity, inefficiency, and poor adaptability to dynamic conditions. This study proposes a tuning-free LLM framework that integrates domain knowledge through dynamic prompts and human-in-the-loop feedback, enabling automated code generation for real-time driver assessment. This framework contains three main parts: constraint-driven prompt engineering dynamically generates structured prompts by integrating task semantics and domain knowledge, and a modular code synthesis produces executable code via stepwise generation, separating data processing, feature calculation, and rule application into modular components while embedding domain-specific constraints. Furthermore, a human-in-the-loop optimization workflow is introduced to validate outputs through automated execution and human feedback, refining prompts and code iteratively until convergence. This study bridges LLMs' linguistic capabilities with domain-specific problem-solving, achieving an average of 85.9% consistency with numerical simulations while significantly reducing expert dependency.
ER  - 

