AB  - Generative AI (GenAI) is redefining how programming is taught and learned. GenAI can perform various programming tasks such as coding and debugging. This research examines students’ experiences with ChatGPT-4 and Microsoft Copilot in programming education. A mixed-methods approach was employed to collect both quantitative and qualitative data through programming exercises and interviews. The study involved 148 pre-university students who used both GenAI tools to complete four types of programming tasks: 72 completed Code Generation, 78 completed Code Explanation, 63 completed Bug Fixing and 68 completed Partial Code Completion. A total of 21 students were involved in the follow-up interview. Data analysis revealed that (1) students who adopted both GenAI tools performed better in programming; though the result was not statistically significant (2) major themes revealed the strengths and weaknesses of ChatGPT-4 in learning programming. The findings underscore the importance of prompt engineering, and the skills required as a navigator. This study suggests a wider implication of programming education, highlighting the need for teaching essential skills required for effective use of GenAI tools instead of drawing a definite conclusion about evolving technologies. Program developers must adapt alongside evolving GenAI tools, suggesting the need for versatile and sustainable education.
ER  - 

