AB  - The emergence of generative artificial intelligence tools such as ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot has introduced new possibilities for programming education. However, few published works have described how these tools have been effectively integrated into undergraduate courses, particularly those with analyses grounded in full semesters of classroom experience. This paper addresses this gap by documenting and examining the use of generative AI in an introductory programming course for non-CS STEM undergraduate students. The course was redesigned to emphasize problem understanding, prompt formulation, and evaluation of AI-generated code, rather than focusing solely on manual implementation. The redesign also allowed expanded curriculum coverage, including additional topics not addressed in the traditional offering, such as debugging and testing. We analyze data from two semesters in which generative AI tools (ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot) were incorporated and compare results with two semesters of the traditional course format. The results suggest that the AI-supported format maintained high student engagement in activity submissions throughout the semester, even after students reached the minimum passing grade. Student feedback indicated increased motivation and perceived relevance of the tools, along with new challenges related to debugging and problem decomposition. We conclude with practical recommendations and lessons learned for course planning and assessment design when integrating generative AI into programming education.
